introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do networks move data reliably and securely at global scale?"
  overview: "This unit develops your understanding of network design, communication rules, addressing, and security controls. You will analyze how systems connect, how protocols coordinate data exchange, and how firewall policies reduce risk."
  journey: "You will describe network purpose and characteristics, describe transport/application protocols, describe practical topology use, describe IP addressing models, and discuss firewall effectiveness in realistic deployment contexts."

concepts:
  - title: "Network types and purpose"
    icon: "fa-network-wired"
    content: |
      <p>Networks are structured by scale, scope, and security needs.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>PAN/LAN/WAN:</strong> personal to global connectivity layers.</li>
          <li><strong>VPN:</strong> secure remote access over public infrastructure.</li>
          <li><strong>Core goal:</strong> reliable communication between distributed systems.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Protocols and communication"
    icon: "fa-handshake"
    content: |
      <p>Protocols define predictable behavior across different layers.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>TCP/UDP:</strong> transport choices for reliability vs speed.</li>
          <li><strong>HTTP/HTTPS:</strong> web application communication.</li>
          <li><strong>DHCP:</strong> automatic address assignment.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Topology and addressing"
    icon: "fa-diagram-project"
    content: |
      <p>Architecture and addressing decisions shape performance and manageability.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Topologies:</strong> star, mesh, hybrid use different trade-offs.</li>
          <li><strong>IPv4/IPv6:</strong> addressing formats with different scale limits.</li>
          <li><strong>NAT and private/public ranges:</strong> practical internet connectivity model.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Security controls"
    icon: "fa-shield-halved"
    content: |
      <p>Firewalls enforce policy but are one layer in a wider security posture.</p>
      <ul>
          <li>Rule-based filtering by IP, port, and protocol.</li>
          <li>Protection value depends on rule quality and maintenance.</li>
          <li>Discussion requires both strengths and limits.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "LAN / WAN / PAN / VPN"
    definition: "Network categories defined by geographic scope and communication purpose."
  - term: "Protocol"
    definition: "Set of communication rules that ensures interoperable data exchange."
  - term: "TCP / UDP"
    definition: "Transport protocols prioritizing reliability/order or low-latency speed."
  - term: "HTTP / HTTPS"
    definition: "Web protocols; HTTPS provides encrypted secure communication."
  - term: "DHCP"
    definition: "Protocol assigning IP configuration automatically to client devices."
  - term: "Topology"
    definition: "Physical or logical arrangement of nodes and links in a network."
  - term: "IPv4 / IPv6"
    definition: "Internet addressing standards using 32-bit and 128-bit addresses."
  - term: "Static / Dynamic IP"
    definition: "Fixed manual allocation vs automatically assigned addresses."
  - term: "NAT"
    definition: "Address translation between private local networks and public internet addresses."
  - term: "Firewall"
    definition: "Security mechanism that allows or blocks traffic based on policy rules."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "WiFi automatically means internet"
    correction: "WiFi provides local wireless connectivity; internet access still depends on routing and upstream service."
  - mistake: "TCP and HTTP do the same job"
    correction: "TCP is transport reliability; HTTP/HTTPS are application protocols using transport services."
  - mistake: "A firewall alone makes a network secure"
    correction: "Firewalls are essential but must be combined with patching, access control, and endpoint security."

